# Payment retry policy

POST /payments is retried only for failures that may succeed later: a timeout, a connection reset, or a 429 or 503 response. A 400 or 409 is returned to the caller without another attempt. A retry is safe only because the server deduplicates the write.

Every logical payment gets one idempotency key before the first attempt, and every retry sends the same key:

~~~ts
const idempotencyKey = crypto.randomUUID();

function sleep(milliseconds: number): Promise<void> {
    return new Promise<void>(function wait(resolve) {
        setTimeout(resolve, milliseconds);
    });
}

async function pay(orderId: string, amountCents: number) {
    for (let attempt = 0; attempt < 5; attempt += 1) {
        const controller = new AbortController();
        const timer = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 2_000);
        try {
            const response = await fetch("/payments", {
                method: "POST",
                headers: { "Idempotency-Key": idempotencyKey },
                body: JSON.stringify({ orderId, amountCents }),
                signal: controller.signal,
            });
            if (response.ok) {
                return await response.json();
            }
            if (response.status !== 429 && response.status < 500) {
                throw new Error("permanent failure " + response.status);
            }
        } finally {
            clearTimeout(timer);
        }
        await sleep(2 ** attempt * 100 + Math.random() * 100);
    }
    throw new Error("payment attempts exhausted");
}
~~~

The server stores the idempotency key with the response and replays that response for a duplicate key. That turns at-least-once delivery into one charge: a retry after the first attempt committed returns the stored result instead of charging again.

Reads after a write carry the write's version token, so the client can require read-your-writes. Across replicas, quorum reads and writes must overlap. With N replicas, R + W > N guarantees that a read quorum intersects the latest write quorum, at the cost of latency and of availability during a partition. W = N favors consistency over write availability; R = 1 favors latency over freshness.

Backpressure is part of the contract. When the queue is full the service returns 429 with a Retry-After header instead of buffering without limit. An unbounded retry loop multiplies load exactly when the service is already failing, so the client caps attempts, backs off with jitter, and surfaces exhaustion to the caller.
